1"><p>HTML island</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markdown has no underline syntax.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>mark</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>HTML island</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No Markdown highlight syntax.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>sub</code>, <code>sup</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>HTML island</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No standard Markdown.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>kbd</code>, <code>samp</code>, <code>var</code>, <code>abbr</code>, <code>cite</code>, <code>q</code>, <code>small</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>HTML island</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No Markdown equivalents.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>span</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>HTML island</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Carries attributes/styling Markdown can't hold.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>br</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markdown</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Hard line break.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="3" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Blocks</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>blockquote</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markdown</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>&gt;</code>-prefixed lines.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>pre</code> + <code>code</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markdown</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fenced code block. <code>class="language-…"</code> becomes the info string.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>ul</code>, <code>ol</code>, <code>li</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markdown</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Bulleted / numbered lists. <code>ol start</code> is preserved (GFM).</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>hr</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markdown</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>---</code>.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>dl</code>, <code>dt</code>, <code>dd</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>HTML island</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No standard Markdown for definition lists.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>details</code>, <code>summary</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>HTML island</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>No standard Markdown for disclosure widgets.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>div</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>HTML island</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Generic container; carries attributes/styling.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>section</code>, <code>article</code>, <code>aside</code>, <code>header</code>, <code>footer</code>, <code>nav</code>, <code>main</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>HTML island</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Sectioning wrappers. The <strong>entire subtree</strong> is stored as HTML (see below).</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="3" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Media</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>img</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Conditional</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markdown <code>![alt](src)</code> when bare. Becomes an HTML island when it carries <code>width</code>, <code>height</code>, <code>style</code>, or <code>class</code>, so styling survives.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>figure</code>, <code>figcaption</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>HTML island</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markdown has no figure/caption construct.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>svg</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>HTML island</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Inline SVG passes through unchanged.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="3" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Tables</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>table</code> (simple)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markdown</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>GFM table when rectangular, with a header row, single-span cells, and inline-only cell content.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>table</code> (complex)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>HTML island</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Any <code>colspan</code>/<code>rowspan</code>, block/multi-paragraph cell content, non-rectangular shape, or missing header row keeps the whole table as HTML.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><code>thead</code>, <code>tbody</code>, <code>tfoot</code>, <code>tr</code>, <code>th</code>, <code>td</code>, <code>caption</code>, <code>colgroup</code>, <code>col</code></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Conditional</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Follow the parent table's ruling — Markdown when the table is GFM-expressible, HTML otherwise.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="3" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Interactive controls (HD2-only)</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Checkbox / task list</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markdown</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>GFM <code>- [x]</code> / <code>- [ ]</code>. Stored as <code>&lt;li data-type="task" data-checked&gt;</code>; interactive in the editor. See <a target="_blank" rel="noopener" href="interactive-controls.hd">Interactive Controls</a>.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Radio group (unnamed)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Markdown</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>HD2 syntax <code>- (x)</code> / <code>- ( )</code>. One list = one single-select, deselectable group.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Radio group (named)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>HTML island</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>A <code>data-group</code> name can't live in the paren markers, so a named group is kept as an HTML block.</p></td></tr></tbody></table>

## Cell alignment in Markdown tables

When a simple table is stored as GFM, per-column alignment is preserved through the delimiter row. A header cell whose `align` attribute or `text-align` style is `left`, `center`, or `right` produces `:---`, `:---:`, or `---:` respectively. Other cell attributes (`scope`, `headers`, `class`) are not expressible in GFM and are dropped; if you need them preserved, the table must be a complex (HTML-island) table.

## Two behaviors to keep in mind

### Block wrappers are self-contained HTML

When a sectioning element such as `<section>` becomes an HTML island, its entire contents are stored as HTML rather than as Markdown nested inside the wrapper. Keep prose you want stored as Markdown outside of these wrappers.

### Attributes on Markdown-native elements

Markdown syntax has nowhere to hold `id`, `class`, `style`, or `data-*`. When one of those attributes matters on an otherwise Markdown-native element, promote the element to a form that stays an HTML island — for example a styled `<img>` (which HD2 already keeps as HTML) or wrapping the content in a `<div>`/`<span>`. On a plain heading, paragraph, or list, such attributes are not currently retained through a round-trip.

## Relationship to the HD spec

HD2's boundary is the natural extension of HD's Markdown round-trip table in the [HD Format Specification](../format-spec.hd). In HD that table describes a one-way "Copy as Markdown"; in HD2 the same classification governs how the file is stored on every save. Elements HD marks as *Lossless* are stored as Markdown; those it marks *Raw HTML* become islands; the *Lossy* cases (tables, images) become the **Conditional** rules above.
